Additional trust boundary: canonical external actions are resolved only by the verified host, with exact structured request and exact executor. Agent JSON cannot supply those records, alias identity, NoEffect or EffectConfirmed facts. Key stability does not assert a receiver implements idempotency: dispatch must compare its actual structured request to the approved descriptor/digest; retries require actual no-effect/never-started evidence. Root journal projection includes known effects and unknown actions across old plan versions. New host action IDs for the same canonical operation/recipient/resource-group/payload are rejected conservatively; differing approved requests require distinct explicit host records. Missing roots, scope/hash mismatches and missing journals fail closed.

Input trust boundary: external provenance approval/hash alone does not mean its artifact contract passed. Exact typed external validation receipts establish those checks; ordinary inputs wait for them. Consumed receipts are immutable per consumer attempt, including source kind, contract, producer attempt/hash or approved external scope/hash. Stale history may remain in snapshots but cannot finish the consumer, satisfy acceptance or admit downstream work. This is actual pure-algorithm enforcement, with actual Store transaction and host execution integration still remaining.

